<%# The reaction line: what people said about a posted thing, and the two controls for saying
    something yourself.

    Locals:
      thing     what is posted — anything including Reactable
      page      the account whose page this is (for the URL, and for "is this mine?")
      kind      the name the routes use for this thing: "page", "showcase", "build"…
      anchor    the id this line lives under, and the wall it belongs to

    One small affordance, repeated, is the whole design. A page can carry a rice, several
    shots, a build, demos and links; a labelled control per feature would make the page a
    control panel. This is a score and two glyphs — and because it is one partial, every
    artifact on the site is reacted to in exactly the same way.

    The wall is the sibling partial. Its summary carries the reply count, so the invitation
    to write sits on the wall itself rather than being repeated here. %>
<% mine = thing.respond_to?(:owner) ? thing.owner == current_user : false %>
<% your_reaction = signed_in? && !mine ? current_user.reaction_to(thing) : nil %>

<div class="reaction-line flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3" id="<%= anchor %>-reactions">
  <span class="reaction-score fact-value text-lg <%= thing.score.negative? ? "text-zinc-400" : "text-amber-300" %>"
        title="what people said about this"><%= thing.score %></span>

  <% if signed_in? && !mine %>
    <%# Pressing what you already pressed takes the reaction back, the way it did on these
        pages. Without that there is no way to un-react, and a mis-click is permanent. %>
    <%= button_to (your_reaction == Rating::LIKE ? "★" : "☆"),
          rate_post_path(page, kind, thing.id, score: Rating::LIKE, anchor: anchor),
          class: "reaction #{'reaction-on' if your_reaction == Rating::LIKE}",
          title: your_reaction == Rating::LIKE ? "you liked this — press to take it back" : "like this",
          form_class: "inline" %>
    <%= button_to (your_reaction == Rating::DISLIKE ? "▼" : "▽"),
          rate_post_path(page, kind, thing.id, score: Rating::DISLIKE, anchor: anchor),
          class: "reaction #{'reaction-on' if your_reaction == Rating::DISLIKE}",
          title: your_reaction == Rating::DISLIKE ? "you disliked this — press to take it back" : "dislike this",
          form_class: "inline" %>
  <% elsif !signed_in? %>
    <%= link_to "sign in to react", new_session_path, class: "label hover:text-zinc-200" %>
  <% end %>
</div>
